# PART B — Chrome / ChromeOS

## B1. "Chrome profiles are not a security boundary" — the exact statement does NOT exist

**NEGATIVE FINDING (measured, not assumed).** I fetched the canonical Chromium security FAQ raw (`?format=TEXT`, base64-decoded, 82,581 bytes) from
https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/docs/security/faq.md
and grepped the full text. The word "profile" appears **7 times only**, at lines 695–700, 1280, 1285, 1289, 1291. **There is no sentence anywhere in the FAQ asserting that Chrome profiles are not a security boundary.** `grep -i "security boundary"` returns 5 hits, none about profiles.

https://www.chromium.org/Home/chromium-security/security-faq/ is a stub that points at the source-tree FAQ; it contains no profile or boundary text. (Verified by fetch.)

**What the FAQ actually says instead** — the functional equivalent, expressed as a threat-model exclusion:

> "### Why aren't physically-local attacks in Chrome's threat model?
> We consider these attacks outside Chrome's threat model, because there is no way for Chrome (or any application) to defend against a malicious user who has managed to log into your device as you, or who can run software with the privileges of your operating system user account. Such an attacker can modify executables and DLLs, change environment variables like `PATH`, change configuration files, read any data your user account owns, email it to themselves, and so on. Such an attacker has total control over your device, and nothing Chrome can do would provide a serious guarantee of defense. This problem is not special to Chrome — all applications must trust the physically-local user."

And the mitigation Chromium itself prescribes — note it delegates separation to the **OS account**, not to Chrome profiles:

> "*    If you share your computer with other people, take advantage of your operating system's ability to manage multiple login accounts, and use a distinct account for each person. For guests, Chrome OS has a built-in Guest account for this purpose."

Related exclusions in the same file:

> "Enterprise policies applying to running, enterprise-enrolled Chrome instances are not by default a security boundary." (line 402) — with the explicit exception: "ChromeOS is an exception to this. On ChromeOS, Chrome integrates more deeply with the host operating system and is able to provide stronger guarantees about policies. Therefore, an enterprise policy bypass by a local user of a ChromeOS device may still be a security bug."

> "On an unmanaged machine, Chrome profiles can be managed via cloud policy if users sign into Chrome using a managed account… the Chrome enterprise administrator should have privileges only to *view and mutate state within the profile that they administer*. Any access outside that profile requires end-user consent." — this is the **only** place the FAQ treats a profile as a scoping unit, and it scopes an *admin*, not an attacker.

**Incognito** (same file, line 426):
> "Bugs in Incognito mode are tracked as privacy bugs, not security bugs."
> "When in Incognito mode, Chrome does not store any new history, cookies, or other state in non-volatile storage. However, Incognito windows will be able to access some previously-stored state, such as browsing history."
> "Note that Incognito mode **provides no protection** if the system has already been compromised as described above."

**Passwords at rest per platform** (line ~1270), directly relevant to profile-directory separation:
> "On Windows, Chrome uses App-Bound encryption to store them on disk encrypted with a key only accessible to the Chrome process as well as admin processes."
> "On Linux… it has switched to storing the credentials in 'Login Data' in the Chrome user's profile directory, but encrypted on disk with a key that is then stored in the user's Gnome Secret Service or KWallet. **If there is no available Secret Service or KWallet, the data is not encrypted when stored.**"
> "On ChromeOS passwords are only obfuscated since all profile data is encrypted by the OS."

### The vendor statement that DOES exist — Chrome Help, not Chromium
https://support.google.com/chrome/answer/2364824 ("Manage Chrome with multiple profiles"). Literal sentences extracted from the served HTML:

> "What others can see when you share Chrome — Only share your device with people you trust."
> "If someone has your device, they can switch to any other Chrome profile on it."
> "If they open your Chrome profile, they can see info like what websites you visited."

That is the closest thing to an official "profiles are not a security boundary" — it is a **CLAIM in consumer help copy**, stated as a UX warning, not as a threat-model rule.

### The boundaries Chromium DOES name
https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/docs/security/web-platform-security-guidelines.md
> "The security team maintains several security boundaries in the WebPlatform: origin, site, secure contexts, cross-origin isolated contexts."
> "The origin is the security boundary we aim to defend."
> "The origin is the primary security boundary of the web, as per the Same-origin policy."
> "User activation is not a security boundary, it is a way to protect users from abusive UX behavior from sites."

Site Isolation (https://www.chromium.org/Home/chromium-security/site-isolation/) defines the process boundary as "the scheme and registered domain name, including the public suffix, but ignoring subdomains, port, or path", ensuring "pages from different websites are always put into different processes". **The enumerated list of maintained boundaries — origin, site, secure context, COI — contains no "profile" entry.** That absence is the finding.

## B2. ChromeOS multi-sign-in — vendor caveats

- https://support.google.com/chrome/a/answer/6023717 → **404 (NOT REACHED)**, both via WebFetch and curl. Also 404: `.../a/answer/9722661`, `.../a/answer/6178258`, `.../a/answer/7130158`. The admin-side multi-sign-in article is not at any URL I could resolve.
- https://support.google.com/chromebook/answer/6088201 → REACHED. Quoted caveats:
  > "You'll receive a reminder to use the feature only with people you trust."
  > "All active people share some settings, such as networks. Other settings, like profile pictures and bookmarks, are specific to each person."
  > "The Google Play Store and any downloaded Android apps are only available for the account that is signed in first. To use them with a different account, sign out of all your accounts and sign in with the account you want to use."
  > "If you're signed in to multiple Google Accounts on your Chromebook, you can't sign out one by one. You'll need to sign out of all accounts at the same time."

  **Google publishes NO cryptographic or data-isolation claim for multi-sign-in.** The only security framing is "use with people you trust" — i.e. the same social-trust framing as Chrome profiles. Networks are explicitly *shared* state. The all-or-nothing sign-out means one user's session lifetime is coupled to every co-signed user's.
- ChromiumOS design doc for multiple sign-in: https://www.chromium.org/chromium-os/chromiumos-design-docs/multiple-sign-in/ → **404, NOT REACHED**.
- MultiProfileUserBehavior policy description at chromeenterprise.google/policies/ → page is a JS shell; content not served to fetch. **NOT REACHED.**
- Corroborating source-tree artifact: cryptohome's own filesystem layout doc marks the multi-user bind mount **deprecated**:
  > "`/home/chronos/u-<s_h_o_u>`: bind mount of `/home/.shadow/<s_h_o_u>/mount/user` used for multi-user support. (deprecated)"
  https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform2/+/HEAD/cryptohome/docs/filesystem_layout.md

## B3. ChromeOS guest mode + cryptohome encryption

Guest mode, from the **primary source-tree doc** (stronger than the help center, which Google rate-limited me off — support.google.com/chromebook/answer/1057090 returned a `/sorry/` CAPTCHA redirect, **NOT REACHED**):

https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform2/+/HEAD/cryptohome/docs/mount.md
> "### Ephemeral mount — Ramfs based vault, which is torn down upon session termination. Doesn't have any persistently stored encryption/credentials data."
> "### Guest mount (public session) — The same with ephemeral, except it is not tied to a particular gaia id. Do not confuse with 'public mount'"
> "### Public mount (kiosk session) — This is a regular persistent mount with the difference, that the credentials for it are not supplied, but derived from the user name (application id)."

So guest data is **ramfs, never written to disk**; kiosk ("public mount") is **persistent with credentials derived from the app id** — i.e. no user secret, a designed weak point.

Filesystem encryption mechanism, current state (https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform2/+/HEAD/cryptohome/docs/filesystem_layout.md):
> "The underlying filesystem mechanisms are using one of the following: `ecryptfs` (deprecated, used on old kernels), fscrypt (current, v1 is used on 4.x boards and v2 is used on 5.4+ boards) and lvm+dmcrypt (upcoming vault management and encryption mechanism). The filesystem encryption keys are themselves encrypted via various mechanisms."

Mount sequence, per the same doc: keys are inserted into **the kernel keyring** for persistent vaults, an out-of-process mounter performs the mount, then PKCS11 token, lock-screen verifier and WebAuthn token are initialized.

Original design doc (https://www.chromium.org/chromium-os/chromiumos-design-docs/protecting-cached-user-data/) — CLAIM/DESIGN, predates dircrypto:
- per-user "vault" directory + keyset created at first login; directory named by "a hash of the user name with a salt value", mounted via eCryptfs at login
- key derivation: "SHA-256 digest of a user-specific salt concatenated with the user's passphrase. The first 128 bits of the digest are used as the user's 'weak password hash.'"
- TPM: system-wide RSA key wrapped with the TPM Storage Root Key; "current TPMs can take around half a second for a private key operation" — rate-limiting is the anti-brute-force mechanism
- **explicitly out of scope**: remote compromise of a currently-logged-in user, running machines with screen lock disabled, cold-boot key extraction from RAM, root-partition compromise
- **not encrypted**: "shared Operating System files and programs"
- guest: "While it does not provide access to the encrypted user data, and the session history is not stored, it does always allow use of the system."

**Verified Access**: https://developers.google.com/chrome/verified-access/overview reached but thin — it attests "policy compliance and (optionally) the identity of the client device". **It does not distinguish device- from user-level attestation and publishes no limitations on that overview page.** Treat "Verified Access proves *which user*" as unsupported by the vendor doc.

## B4. CVEs — ChromeOS/profile

NVD REST API, `keywordSearch`:

**cryptohome (3 total):**
| CVE | Published | CVSS | What crosses |
|---|---|---|---|
| CVE-2017-15405 | 2019-01-09 | 7.0 HIGH | "Inappropriate symlink handling and a race condition in the stateful recovery feature… could lead to a persistance established by a malicious code running with root privileges in cryptohomed" — <61.0.3163.113 |
| CVE-2020-16020 | 2021-01-08 | 8.8 HIGH | "Inappropriate implementation in cryptohome… allowed a remote attacker who had compromised the browser process to **bypass discretionary access control** via a malicious file." <87.0.4280.66 |
| CVE-2021-21117 | 2021-02-09 | 7.8 HIGH | "Insufficient policy enforcement in Cryptohome… allowed a local attacker to perform **OS-level privilege escalation** via a crafted file." <88.0.4324.96 |

**"chrome profile" (13 total; Chrome-relevant ones):** CVE-2021-37988 (2021-11-02, 8.8, UAF in Profiles), CVE-2022-0456 (2022-04-05, 8.8, UAF via profile destruction), CVE-2022-1145 (2022-07-23, 7.5, UAF in Extensions via profile destruction), **CVE-2022-1862 (2022-07-27, 6.5 MEDIUM — "Inappropriate implementation in Extensions… allowed an attacker who convinced a user to install a malicious extension to *bypass profile restrictions* via a crafted HTML page")**, CVE-2022-4191 (2022-11-30, 8.8, UAF in Sign-In via profile destruction), CVE-2022-4440 (2022-12-14, 8.8), CVE-2023-5472 (2023-10-25, 8.8), CVE-2023-5854 (2023-11-01, 8.8), CVE-2025-1916 (2025-03-05, 8.8, UAF in Profiles via malicious extension).

CVE-2022-1862 is the one that names a **profile restriction bypass** as the impact — and Chromium rated it only "Medium", consistent with profiles not being a hardened boundary.

**"multi-profile"**: 1 result, CVE-2025-47342, a Qualcomm QHS DOS — **irrelevant. Negative finding: NVD holds no "multi-profile Chrome" cross-profile vulnerability class.**

**"Chrome OS user"**: 66 results, dominated by generic Chrome renderer/UAF bugs. Notable ChromeOS-local ones: CVE-2011-2169 (2011-05-24, 7.2 — "allows local users to gain privileges by creating a /var/lib/chromeos-aliases.conf file"), CVE-2013-0927 (2013-04-10, 7.5 — Pango loads `.pangorc` from the user's home directory), CVE-2022-0107 (2022-02-12, 8.8, UAF in File Manager API), CVE-2022-0808 (2022-04-05, 8.8, UAF in Chrome OS Shell). **None is a cross-*user* data-read on ChromeOS.** The cryptohome vault model appears to hold in the CVE record.

