Vector on 34484: **local, low privilege required, no user interaction, C/I/A all High** — a *logged-in standard user* reaches SYSTEM, which subsumes every other user's profile. This is the measured shape of "user boundary held by ACLs on a profile directory + a service that follows symlinks."

**Windows DPAPI (4 results, only 2 are Microsoft's):**
- **CVE-2022-34723**, 2022-09-13, **5.5 CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N** — "Windows DPAPI Information Disclosure Vulnerability". Local, low-priv, **Confidentiality High** — an unprivileged local account reading DPAPI-protected data.
- **CVE-2023-36004**, 2023-12-12, 7.5 CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:R/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H — "Windows DPAPI Spoofing Vulnerability".
- CVE-2016-5720 (Skype `dpapi.dll` planting) and CVE-2026-18759 (third-party) are not Windows DPAPI itself.

**Credential Guard (12 results, all 2022 + one 2024):** CVE-2022-21921 (4.4, SFB), CVE-2022-30150 (7.5, Remote Credential Guard EOP), CVE-2022-22031 (7.8, "Domain-joined Public Key EOP"), CVE-2022-34704 (**4.7, CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:H/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N — Information Disclosure**), CVE-2022-34710 (5.5 info disc), CVE-2022-34712 (5.5 info disc), CVE-2022-34705 / CVE-2022-34711 / CVE-2022-35771 (7.8 EOP), CVE-2022-34709 (6.0 SFB), CVE-2022-35822 (7.1 SFB), CVE-2024-26237 (7.8 EOP). The August 2022 cluster (8 CVEs in one Patch Tuesday) is a measured burst against the VBS credential isolation boundary — three of them **local low-priv reads of high-confidentiality data**.

**Windows Hello (17 results):** CVE-2021-34466 (2021-07-16, 5.7 SFB), CVE-2021-42288 (2021-11-10, 5.7), CVE-2022-35797 (2022-08-09, 6.1), CVE-2023-32018 (2023-06-14, 7.8 RCE), CVE-2025-26635 (2025-04-08, 6.5 "Weak authentication… bypass a security feature over a network"), CVE-2025-26644 (2025-04-08, 5.1 — "**Automated recognition mechanism with inadequate detection or handling of adversarial input perturbations… allows an unauthorized attacker to perform spoofing**"), CVE-2025-47969 (2025-06-10, 4.4 local info disclosure), CVE-2025-53139 (2025-10-14, 7.7 cleartext transmission), CVE-2026-20804 / CVE-2026-20852 (2026-01-13, both 7.7, "Incorrect privilege assignment… tampering locally"), CVE-2026-27906 (2026-04-14, 4.4), CVE-2026-27928 (2026-04-14, **8.7, unauthorized attacker, security-feature bypass over a network**), CVE-2026-61928 (2026-08-11, 5.5, "**Cleartext storage of sensitive information in Windows Hello**"). Plus third-party sensor failures that break the Hello trust chain: CVE-2023-50430 (Goodix, Dell Inspiron 15, SDCP not followed on Linux enrollment), CVE-2024-23592 (Lenovo/Synaptics fingerprint replay, physical access), CVE-2024-0454 (ELAN Match-on-Chip, "valid SID leakage and enumeration with spoof sensor").

**Windows Cloud Files Mini Filter Driver (cldflt.sys)** — the densest single cross-boundary family, 20 Microsoft CVEs found, essentially one per quarter for four years: CVE-2020-17103, CVE-2020-17134, CVE-2020-17136 (2020-12-10; 7.0/7.8/7.8), CVE-2021-31969 (2021-06-08, 7.8), CVE-2021-33784 (2021-07-14, 7.8), CVE-2021-40475 (2021-10-13, 5.5 **info disclosure**), CVE-2022-23286 (2022-03-09, 7.0), CVE-2022-35757 (2023-05-31, 7.3), CVE-2023-29361 (2023-06-14, 7.0), CVE-2023-33155 (2023-07-11, 7.8), CVE-2023-36904 (2023-08-08, 7.8), CVE-2023-35355 (2023-09-12, 7.8), CVE-2023-36036 (2023-11-14, 7.8 — **exploited in the wild**, per MSRC's own labeling of that month), CVE-2023-36696 (2023-12-12, 7.8), CVE-2024-21310 (2024-01-09, 7.8), CVE-2024-26160 (2024-03-12, 5.5 info disc), CVE-2024-30034 (2024-05-14, 5.5 info disc), CVE-2024-30085 (2024-06-11, 7.8), CVE-2024-38215 (2024-08-13, 7.8), CVE-2024-49114 (2024-12-12, 7.8), CVE-2025-21271 (2025-01-14, 7.8). This is the **OneDrive/cloud-placeholder filter driver** — precisely the component Shared PC mode's `EnableSharedPCModeWithOneDriveSync` turns on.

**"%TEMP% cross user" / "another user profile" as keyword searches: NEGATIVE.** 53 and 31 results respectively, essentially all web-app profile-tampering CVEs and unrelated symlink-in-`/tmp` Unix bugs. NVD's keyword index does not carry a "cross-user Windows temp" class. Two incidental hits worth naming: **CVE-2007-0351** (2007-01-19, XP/Server 2003, "do not properly handle user logoff, which might allow local users to gain the privileges of a previous system user") and **CVE-2019-9377** (Android FingerprintService, "possible bypass for operating system protections that isolate user profiles from each other due to a missing permission check") — the latter is the exact phrasing that does *not* exist for Windows.

**The strongest measured cross-account read on Windows** — fetched by cveId:
> **CVE-2021-36934** (HiveNightmare/SeriousSAM), published 2021-07-22, **CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H = 7.8**: "An elevation of privilege vulnerability exists because of **overly permissive Access Control Lists (ACLs) on multiple system files, including the Security Accounts Manager (SAM) database**."

Any non-admin interactive user could read every local account's password hashes from VSS shadow copies. That is a total collapse of the Windows multi-user boundary, caused by **file ACLs alone**, shipped for months in Windows 10/11. It is the empirical answer to "how strong is Windows local user separation": as strong as the ACL that was last set on the file.

## D7. DPAPI cross-user decryption — measured, and it is by design

**Primary source, MEASURED demonstration:** harmj0y (Will Schroeder, SpecterOps), "Operational Guidance for Offensive User DPAPI Abuse", **2018-08-22**, https://blog.harmj0y.net/redteaming/operational-guidance-for-offensive-user-dpapi-abuse/ (the posts.specterops.io mirror returned no output — **NOT REACHED**; the harmj0y.net original is live).

Exact claims from the post:
> "Domain user master keys are also protected with a domain-wide *backup* DPAPI key" — and "*this backup key doesn't change!*"

He frames it as intended architecture: "a failsafe in case users change/lose their passwords, and to support various smart cards' functionality."

The measured chain, verbatim commands:
1. `lsadump::backupkeys /system:<DOMAIN CONTROLLER> /export`
2. `dpapi::masterkey /in:b8854128-023c-433d-aac9-232b4bca414c /pvk:ntds_capi_0_32d021e7-ab1c-4877-af06-80473ca3e4d8.pvk`
3. `dpapi::chrome /in:Cookies /masterkey:f35cfc2b44aedd7…`

**Result: the author decrypted user "harmj0y"'s Chrome cookie database offline, from the domain backup key alone, without that user's password.** That is a MEASURED cross-user decryption, and step 3 lands squarely on Part B — the DPAPI-protected Chrome profile directory is readable by anyone holding a key the user never controlled and cannot rotate.

**Vendor confirmation of the mechanism** (CLAIM, but it is Microsoft describing its own protocol) — [MS-BKRP] BackupKey Remote Protocol, revision 27.0 dated 2024-04-23, https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-bkrp/:
> "This protocol encrypts secret values (such as cryptographic keys) so they can be backed up to storage that is not specially protected, and enables decryption of such values if recovery is necessary."

The deep-link `.../ms-bkrp/044ea320-...` 404'd; the landing page above is the reachable canonical. Full spec PDF: https://winprotocoldocs-bhdugrdyduf5h2e4.b02.azurefd.net/MS-BKRP/%5bMS-BKRP%5d.pdf (not fetched — **NOT REACHED**, it is a binary PDF).

**Academic corroboration: NOT REACHED.** OpenAlex is hard-exhausted for the day — `{"error":"Rate limit exceeded","message":"Insufficient budget… Resets at midnight UTC"}`. The Picod/Bursztein DPAPI offline-recovery line of work could not be confirmed and I will not cite it from memory. HN Algolia returned 4 hits for "DPAPI backup key" but every record had `title: null, url: null` — comment hits with no story attached; **no usable practitioner discussion found.**

---

## Cross-cutting synthesis

1. **Chrome profiles**: the "not a security boundary" statement is folklore in the exact form the brief assumed. What exists is (a) a threat-model *exclusion* of the whole local-attacker class, (b) an enumerated list of maintained boundaries — origin, site, secure context, cross-origin-isolated — that omits profiles, and (c) a consumer help-page warning. Chromium's own prescribed remedy for a shared machine is **the OS account**, not a second Chrome profile.

2. **That remedy is itself measured-weak.** Windows local user separation rests on file ACLs (CVE-2021-36934 read every SAM hash from a standard account), on a symlink-following profile service (11 ProfSvc EOPs, 2015–2022, still recurring after two patches on the same chain), and on DPAPI — whose domain backup key decrypts *any* domain user's Chrome cookies by design, demonstrated end-to-end in 2018 and unchanged since ("this backup key doesn't change!").

3. **ChromeOS is the only one of the three that separates users with a cryptographic mechanism rather than an ACL** — per-user fscrypt/dmcrypt vault, TPM-wrapped keys, ramfs-only guest — and its CVE record reflects that: 3 cryptohome CVEs ever, none a cross-user data read. But **multi-sign-in undoes it at the policy layer** (shared networks, single joint sign-out, Play Store bound to the first account, and no published isolation claim at all), and **kiosk "public mounts" derive their vault key from the application id, not a secret.**

4. **Shared PC mode is the mirror image of the assumption behind it**: it is a disk-reclamation and account-lifecycle feature (25%/50% thresholds, 30-day inactivity, a nightly `Account Cleanup` task), and it *explicitly enables* "Allow a Windows app to share application data between users" while disabling Windows Hello, biometrics, and PIN sign-in. It deletes Guest/Kiosk profiles at sign-out and **leaves every pre-existing and manually-added local account permanently**. Microsoft publishes no statement of what it fails to isolate.
